Audiohooks: Rebuild the list's translators when its sample rate changes

The audiohook list of a channel converts the frames it is given to signed
linear at the list's internal sample rate, and back, through two cached
translation paths. The paths were rebuilt only when the frame's format
changed, not when the rate did. The rate rises to 16 kHz while a spied
channel sits in a mixing bridge with a 16 kHz channel, and is reset to
8 kHz by every attach and removal of an audiohook; after such a reset the
cached path kept producing 16 kHz frames while the list and its hooks
believed in 8 kHz. Whisper audio from a Snoop channel was then taken from
an 8 kHz factory in 16 kHz portions: present in every other frame and at
double speed, which for a tone is not heard at all. Observed on Asterisk
22.11.0 when a whispering Snoop was removed and created again on a channel
whose recording Snoop (spy) kept the audiohook list alive in between.

A path now remembers the slin format it was built for, and is rebuilt when
either end changes; the list's destructor releases that format.

Not in Asterisk as of 22.12.0-rc2: branch 22 and master rebuild the path on
a format change only. Written for the Gabion telephony node.
